MOVES = [
("Move 1: underline the nouns. Every noun with its own state becomes a class.",
 "Reading the paragraph again: <b>players</b> take <b>turns</b> on a <b>board</b> of cells; a <b>die</b> decides how "
 "far; <b>snakes</b> and <b>ladders</b> move you again; somebody <b>wins</b>. The game holds every token's position, "
 "the turn order and the status, all of which change every turn: a class, and the only thing here that owns anything "
 "that moves. The board holds its size and its jumps and never changes after it is built: a class. A snake and a "
 "ladder are the same object &mdash; a one-way teleport from one cell to another &mdash; and the only difference is "
 "whether the end is below the start, which is a sign, not a reason for two classes; so there is one <code>Jump</code> "
 "and a <code>kind()</code> that works it out. A player has an id and a name and nothing that moves, so it is a record "
 "and the position lives on the game: that one decision is what makes the concurrency answer easy later. A turn is "
 "worth keeping, so it is a record too. The dice and the rules have no state at all, so they are interfaces.", 1),
("Move 2: for every verb, ask which class holds the state it touches. That class gets the method.",
 "\"Throw a number\" touches only the randomness, so it belongs to the dice: <code>dice.roll()</code>. \"Turn a throw "
 "into a landing cell\" touches nothing at all &mdash; it reads three numbers and returns one &mdash; so it is a pure "
 "rule, <code>rule.landingCell(from, roll, size)</code>. \"Teleport a landing to a jump's end\" reads the jump table, "
 "so it belongs to the board: <code>board.jumpAt(cell)</code>. \"Move a token and pass the turn\" touches the "
 "positions, the queue, the status and the log at once; only the game sees all four, so <code>game.roll(playerId)</code> "
 "is the orchestrator and everything else it calls is small. Notice what is not a verb of its own: winning. Winning is "
 "a comparison the game makes on the cell the token came to rest on, which is why it is one line and not a class.", 2),
("Move 3: every rule the interviewer can change mid-round goes behind an interface and is handed in.",
 "Three things will change, and you can name them before they are asked. How you throw: one die today, three dice or "
 "a loaded die tomorrow, a scripted die in every test. What happens when a throw would pass the last cell: stay put "
 "today, bounce back tomorrow, reach-or-pass the day after. Whether the maximum grants another turn: no today, yes "
 "with a cap tomorrow. Each becomes a one-method interface the game is <i>given</i> by its builder and never "
 "constructs. This is where the patterns come from, not the other way round: a swappable rule behind an interface is "
 "<b>Strategy</b>; a rule that wraps another rule and adds to it is <b>Decorator</b>, and here it is "
 "<code>CappedExtraTurn</code>, which adds \"but not more than three in a row\" to any extra-turn rule, including ones "
 "written next year. A game that announces \"a turn happened\" without knowing what a screen is, is <b>Observer</b>. "
 "I do them; I do not announce them.", 3),
("Move 4: state that many callers change at the same time gets one owner and one lock.",
 "The single-process version of this game needs no lock at all, and saying so is worth a mark: one loop mutates "
 "everything, so adding <code>synchronized</code> would be cargo cult. The moment it is an app it changes. Bob has the "
 "game open on his phone and his tablet and presses roll on both. Both read \"it is Bob's turn\", both roll, both move "
 "his token: one turn, two moves &mdash; or worse, the queue rotates twice and somebody is skipped. So checking whose "
 "turn it is, throwing, moving and rotating must be <i>one</i> step, in the class that owns all of it: the game. The "
 "lock is per <i>game</i>, not per server, which is the whole trick &mdash; a million tables play in parallel and "
 "never wait for each other, while the four people at one table are serialised for half a microsecond. Anything that "
 "only listens (the commentary, the screens) is called after the lock is released, never inside it.", 4),
("Move 5: for each collection, ask what question is asked of it, and pick the shape that answers in O(1).",
 "\"What is on the cell I landed on?\" is a map from cell to jump. That is the shape worth defending: a hundred-cell "
 "board has about twenty interesting cells, so an array of a hundred <code>Cell</code> objects stores eighty empty "
 "ones to answer a question a map answers in one hop. (The honest counter-argument: if a UI arrives and every cell "
 "needs a picture and a colour, a dense array becomes the better home &mdash; say that and move on.) \"Whose turn is "
 "it?\" is a deque: <code>peek</code> to see, <code>poll</code> then <code>addLast</code> to rotate, all O(1), and the "
 "code reads like the domain. \"Where is this token?\" is a map from player id to cell. \"How many snakes and ladders "
 "were taken?\" is an <code>EnumMap</code> keyed by the jump kind, which is an array behind the scenes. \"How did we "
 "get here?\" is an append-only list of turns, which is the audit log and the replay input at the same time.", 5),
("Move 6: anything with a life cycle is a state machine, and the order of operations is part of the design.",
 "A game is WAITING when it is built, IN_PROGRESS after <code>start()</code>, and FINISHED the instant a token rests "
 "on the last cell; a roll in the wrong state is refused rather than quietly ignored. Writing that down forces the "
 "question the interviewer is about to ask: what if something goes wrong half way through a turn? The answer is an "
 "order. Check the state and whose turn it is first, <i>before the die is touched</i>, so a phone that rolls out of "
 "turn costs the game nothing &mdash; not a log row, not even a random number, which is exactly what keeps a seeded "
 "game reproducible no matter how many stray requests arrive. Then throw, ask the move rule for the landing cell, "
 "apply at most one jump, and decide the win on the resting cell so that a ladder onto the last cell wins too. Only "
 "then write: the position, the counters, the log row, the status. Rotate the queue last, so nobody can ever read a "
 "token that has moved while its owner is still shown as due. And the board itself is validated once, in "
 "<code>build()</code> &mdash; two jumps on one cell, an endpoint off the board, a snake on the winning cell are all "
 "refused there &mdash; so the turn loop never has to check a layout it was handed.", 6),
("Move 7: yes, the lock makes one table's turns happen one at a time. Ask for how long, and what is inside it.",
 "Inside the lock there is a queue peek and one string comparison, the throw itself (arithmetic on a seed), one map "
 "get for the position, the move rule (three numbers in, one out), one map get for the jump, one map put, one deque "
 "rotation and one list append: about ten operations, roughly half a microsecond, and not one of them can block. "
 "Everything slow is outside. The commentary and the websocket push to four phones &mdash; tens of milliseconds "
 "&mdash; happen after the unlock, inside a try/catch, so a screen that has crashed cannot stop the game. When four "
 "phones press roll in the same millisecond, only one of them is going to do any work: the other three discover they "
 "are not on turn after two comparisons, in about a tenth of a microsecond, and go away. So the answer to \"is "
 "everything now one by one?\" is: per table, for half a microsecond, and no human being can tell.", 7),
("Move 8: say the arithmetic, then name the ladder.",
 "Four people at a table throw a die every three seconds or so, which is a third of a roll per second against a lock "
 "held for half a microsecond: the lock is busy about two hundred-thousandths of one per cent of the time. Scale it "
 "the other way and it is still fine, because the lock is per game: a million live tables doing three hundred "
 "thousand rolls a second between them is about a sixth of one core's worth of locked work, spread over a million "
 "separate locks that never meet. Then the ladder, in the order you would climb it. Keep the screens and the "
 "persistence outside the lock, which this code already does. If you do not want a thread parked on a lock at all, "
 "replace the gate with a turn token: whose turn it is lives in an <code>AtomicReference</code> and a player moves by "
 "winning a compare-and-set, so a roll out of turn is refused without blocking. And beyond one process, the game "
 "becomes a row and the turn becomes <code>UPDATE game SET turn_no = turn_no + 1 WHERE game_id = ? AND turn_no = ?</code>, "
 "which is the database performing exactly the same compare-and-set, with the request id as the idempotency key. Say "
 "the arithmetic first: climbing the ladder without it is complexity nobody asked for.", 8),
("Move 9: list what can go wrong, and write the test for each before the interview is over.",
 "Two phones rolling for the same player at the same instant (one lock, and the turn checked inside it). A phone "
 "rolling out of turn (refused before the die, so it costs nothing &mdash; the test asserts that the number of rolls "
 "taken out of the dice equals the number of turns played). A ladder whose top is a snake head (one jump per landing, "
 "so the token rests on the ladder top). A throw that would pass the last cell (wasted, and nobody wins by "
 "overshooting). A rule that hands back a cell off the board (checked before any write, so the position, the log and "
 "the turn order are all exactly as they were and the client can retry). A board layout that would break the game "
 "(refused in <code>build()</code>, so no Game is ever created from it). A screen that throws while being told "
 "(published after the unlock, in a try/catch). Each of these is a few lines in FailureTests.java; a design that "
 "cannot show its tests is a claim.", 9),
("Move 10: now, and only now, name the patterns. Each one is the result of a move.",
 "Strategy is move 3: the dice, the overshoot rule and the extra-turn rule are the three things that will change, so "
 "each sits behind a one-method interface the game is handed. Decorator is the same move's wrapper, "
 "<code>CappedExtraTurn</code>, which adds a limit to any extra-turn rule instead of copying the counter into each "
 "one. Observer is move 4's rule that a screen must never be inside the lock. State is move 6, the game's life, which "
 "is why a roll after the win is refused instead of quietly appending to a finished game. Command is move 6 as well, "
 "quietly: the <code>Turn</code> record carries who, what they threw, where they came from and where they ended, so "
 "the log is an audit trail, a replay script and an undo log without any extra code. And Builder &mdash; which on most "
 "of these problems is the pattern you should refuse &mdash; genuinely earns its place here: there are seven optional "
 "knobs and four real invariants, <code>build()</code> is the single place they are checked, and because Game's "
 "constructor is package-private an invalid game cannot be constructed at all. Factory has not earned one yet: the "
 "builder's <code>dice(count, faces)</code> is already the registry, and it earns the name the day rules arrive as "
 "strings from configuration. Singleton earned nothing: the server object is handed to its callers, so a test builds a "
 "fresh one. A pattern without a move behind it is decoration.", 10),
("Move 11: run SOLID as a check on the moves, one line each.",
 "S: a class has one reason to change, which move 2 gave you &mdash; the board changes when a landing resolves "
 "differently, the dice when a throw does, the game when the turn loop does, and nobody does two of those. O: a new "
 "behaviour is a new class and one changed line, never an opened one; <code>ReachOrPass</code> is four words of "
 "arithmetic in a new file and the turn loop never learns about it. L: any implementation drops in and nothing asks "
 "which one it got &mdash; the game calls <code>dice.roll()</code>, never \"is this the scripted one?\", which is "
 "exactly what lets a test script the throws. I: five interfaces with one method each, so a fake for a test is a "
 "lambda. D: the game depends on interfaces and is handed the implementations by its builder, which is why a test can "
 "hand it a die that throws 6, 6, 2 and a clock that says last Tuesday.", 11),
("Move 12: every twist the interviewer adds is one of five moves. Say which before you type.",
 "A new rule (bounce back, reach-or-pass, three dice, a loaded die, a bot that picks its dice) is a new class behind "
 "an existing interface plus one line in the builder. Someone new who wants to know (a scoreboard, analytics, a "
 "tournament table) is one more observer; the game and the lock do not change. A new step in a life (miss a turn, a "
 "player quits, a rematch) is one more field and one more checked transition inside <code>roll()</code> &mdash; and "
 "\"miss a turn\" is the one special cell that is <i>not</i> free, because it changes the rotation rather than the "
 "position, which is worth saying out loud rather than pretending. A new invariant across players (no two tokens on "
 "one cell) is the check and the writes inside the <i>same</i> lock, all or nothing. State that must outlive the "
 "process (persist it; two servers) is the positions and the log behind a repository, with the turn applied as a "
 "conditional update on the turn number and the request id as the idempotency key. For all five the turn loop, the "
 "board and the tests do not change; that is the test that the derivation was right. Page 05 has the code for each.", 12),
]
DERIVATION_LEAD = ("Run these on any LLD (parking lot, elevator, Splitwise) and the class diagram, the lock, the tests, the "
 "patterns, SOLID and the answer to every twist fall out in that order; nothing is chosen up front, and nothing is named "
 "before the move that produced it. On this problem two moves do most of the work: move 1, where noticing that a snake "
 "and a ladder are one object deletes a whole hierarchy before it is born, and move 6, where the order inside a turn is "
 "the entire difference between a toy and something you would put four phones in front of.")

