HOW_TO_READ = ('<b>How to read a box.</b> Top: the class name (dashed border = interface; &laquo;enum&raquo; = a fixed '
 'list of values). Middle: its fields, the state it holds. Bottom: its methods. <b>The arrows.</b> Hollow triangle = '
 'implements. Filled diamond = owns: the game owns the board and every turn, and the board owns its jumps. Plain arrow '
 '= references: a turn points at the jump that carried it. Dashed lavender = handed in by the builder. Dotted blue = '
 'notifies &mdash; and the builder is the box that hands the game its rules and its clock. <b>Where state lives:</b> everything that moves is on the <code>Game</code> &mdash; every token\'s '
 'position, the turn queue, the log, the counters, the status &mdash; and all of it is behind one '
 '<code>ReentrantLock</code>, which is the whole concurrency answer in one sentence. The board is fixed once '
 '<code>build()</code> has validated it. A player is an id and a name; a jump is two cells; a turn is what happened. '
 'Notice what is <i>not</i> here: no Snake and no Ladder class, because they are one shape with a sign; no Cell class, '
 'because a cell with nothing on it is just a number; and no position field on Player, because the moment two phones '
 'can roll, shared state belongs to whoever holds the lock.')

# ============================================================ page 04: the code
CODE_INTRO = ('Read it with page 03 open in a second tab if you want the diagram beside it. The green comment above each '
 'class and method says what it does; read only those first for the shape, then the bodies for the mechanics &mdash; and '
 'read <code>Game.roll</code> twice, because the order of its lines is the design. Each copy button copies that whole '
 'file for your IDE. Below Main.java: Extensions.java (every follow-up\'s reference code, with an <code>ExtDemo</code> '
 'main that runs all of it) and FailureTests.java (47 checks in nine blocks; '
 '<code>javac Main.java Extensions.java FailureTests.java &amp;&amp; java FailureTests</code> prints ALL PASS).')

# ============================================================ page 05: follow-ups and practice
IMPLEMENT_CARD = ('<div class="card"><div class="ch"><h3>0 &middot; Implement the system</h3>'
 '<button class="timer" data-min="60">start 60:00</button></div><div class="cb"><div class="prompt">' + PROMPT + '</div>'
 'Before typing, write your six to eight clarifying questions (exact landing and whether jumps chain come first); then '
 'type in the order of Main.java: the two enums, Jump and Player, the Clock, the Dice interface with a standard and a '
 'scripted one, the MoveStrategy interface with its two rules, the ExtraTurnRule with its wrapper, the Turn record, '
 'Board with its validation, then Game with its lock and the order inside <code>roll()</code>, then GameBuilder, then '
 'the thin GameServer, then a main that plays a seeded game and runs the race.</div></div>')

FU = [
("How would you test a game whose every move is a random number? And could a player be a bot that picks its own dice?", "non-functional", 5,
 "The randomness never comes from inside: <code>Dice</code> is an interface and the <code>Random</code> is handed in, "
 "so production passes a seeded one and a test passes <code>ScriptedDice</code>, which replays a fixed sequence and "
 "throws if the test asks for one roll more than it scripted. That is what makes an assertion like \"after a 4 the "
 "token is on the ladder at 4 and therefore rests on 20\" possible at all. The same seam absorbs every other dice "
 "question: two dice, twelve faces, a weighted die, or a bot that looks at the board before it throws &mdash; "
 "<code>BotDice</code> throws one die when a ladder is within six and two when snakes are, and the game cannot tell "
 "the difference.",
 X("a bot that chooses which dice to throw", "special cells")),
("Two phones press roll at the same instant. Prove a token cannot move twice, with a test.", "non-functional", 10,
 "The race lives between \"it is Bob's turn\" and \"Bob has moved\". <code>roll()</code> does the whole thing &mdash; "
 "the state check, the turn check, the throw, the move and the rotation &mdash; inside one lock held by the game, so "
 "no second caller can run in that gap. The proof is two tests. Eight threads wait on a latch and all roll for the "
 "same, correct player: exactly one is accepted, the token has moved exactly once, and the turn has passed exactly "
 "once. Then four hundred attempts from eight threads roll for random players: the log has exactly one row per "
 "accepted roll, the turn numbers run 1..n with no gap, every token starts each turn where its own previous turn left "
 "it, and replaying the log reproduces the live board.",
 T("        // 1. four phones press roll over and over at the same instant.", "        // 3. a ladder lifts")),
("One lock per game. Does that scale, or have you serialised the server?", "non-functional", 5,
 "It scales, and the answer is arithmetic. The locked part is about ten operations &mdash; a queue peek, a string "
 "compare, the throw, two map gets, a map put, a rotation and a list append &mdash; roughly half a microsecond, with "
 "nothing in it that can block. Four people at a table roll about a third of a time a second, so the lock is busy "
 "about two hundred-thousandths of one per cent. And it is per game: a million tables doing three hundred thousand "
 "rolls a second between them is about a sixth of a core, spread over a million separate locks. The ladder, in order: "
 "screens and persistence already run outside the lock; if you want no blocking at all, replace the gate with an "
 "<code>AtomicReference</code> holding whose turn it is and move by winning a compare-and-set; beyond one process, "
 "the game is a row and the turn is a conditional UPDATE on the turn number.",
 X("online play", "persistence")),
("A six rolls again — but not more than three times in a row. Add it without touching Game.", "twist", 10,
 "The extra-turn rule is already an interface, and it is pure: the game hands it the roll and how many extras this "
 "player has had in a row, and it answers yes or no. So \"a six rolls again\" is <code>ExtraTurnOnMax</code>, and the "
 "cap is not a copy of it &mdash; it is a wrapper, <code>CappedExtraTurn</code>, that asks the inner rule and then "
 "refuses once the count reaches the cap. That is the Decorator, and it works on any extra-turn rule ever written. The "
 "game's part is two lines: when the rule says yes it does not rotate the queue and increments the counter, and when "
 "anything else happens it resets the counter to zero. The failure test throws three sixes and asserts the turn passes "
 "on the third.",
 sect(src, "interface ExtraTurnRule {", "record Turn(")),
("Must you land exactly on the last cell, or does reaching it do? They change their mind mid-round.", "functional", 5,
 "Both rules already exist and swapping them is one line in the builder. <code>OvershootStays</code> is the classic: a "
 "throw that would pass the last cell is wasted and the token stays, so you must come to rest exactly on it. "
 "<code>BounceBack</code> sends the surplus steps back off the end. <code>ReachOrPass</code>, in Extensions.java, is "
 "four words of arithmetic: clamp the landing to the last cell. What is worth pointing at is that the win check never "
 "changes &mdash; it is <code>to == board.size()</code>, evaluated on the cell the token came to rest on &mdash; so a "
 "ladder onto the last cell wins as well, and a board is refused at build time if anything starts on that cell.",
 sect(src, "interface MoveStrategy {", "// \"and if you roll a six") + "\n" + X("reach or pass to win", "loaded dice")),
("A rule you were handed gives back a cell that is not on the board. What is the state of the game?", "functional", 10,
 "Exactly what it was. Nothing at all is written until the whole turn has been worked out: the throw, the landing "
 "cell, the jump and the win are computed into local variables, and the landing and the destination are both checked "
 "against the board before the first <code>put</code>. So a broken move rule throws, the token has not moved, the log "
 "has no row, the queue has not rotated and the same player is still due &mdash; the client simply rolls again. The "
 "test hands in a rule that returns <code>size + 99</code> and asserts all four of those. The one thing that is spent "
 "is the die itself, which is honest: the throw already happened, exactly as it does when somebody knocks the board "
 "over after rolling.",
 M("Turn roll(String playerId) {", "Turn forfeitTurn(String playerId)")),
("The screen asks \"what is on cell 47?\" and \"whose turn is it?\" a thousand times a second.", "non-functional", 5,
 "Both are already one hop and no new structure is needed. A landing resolves through <code>Map&lt;Integer, Jump&gt;</code>, "
 "which stores only the twenty cells that do something rather than a hundred objects of which eighty are empty; whose "
 "turn it is, is <code>peek</code> on an <code>ArrayDeque</code>, and rotating is <code>poll</code> then "
 "<code>addLast</code>, both O(1), which is why the turn order is a queue and not a list with an index somebody has to "
 "maintain. A position is a map get. The reads take the game's lock so a caller never sees a half-applied turn, which "
 "costs tens of nanoseconds; the dense-array alternative is worth naming &mdash; if a UI needs a picture per cell, an "
 "array of cells becomes the better home, and nothing else changes.",
 sect(src, "class Board {", "class OutOfTurnException")),
("Special cells: land here and teleport, land there and jump forward, land on that one and miss a turn.", "twist", 10,
 "Two of the three are free and one is not, and saying which is the answer. The game asks the board exactly one "
 "question &mdash; what happens to a token that rests on this cell &mdash; so a teleport cell and a boost cell are a "
 "<code>Board</code> subclass that answers with a synthesised jump, and the turn loop never learns they exist; its "
 "randomness comes from the same injected <code>Random</code> as the dice, or replay would stop working. Miss a turn "
 "is different in kind, because it changes the rotation rather than the position: it needs one field on the game (the "
 "set of players to skip) and one line at the top of <code>roll()</code>. That is move 12's third twist, a new step in "
 "a life, and it costs about three lines.",
 X("special cells", "turn timers")),
("Make it a real online game: many tables, phones rolling out of turn, and a request that gets retried.", "twist", 10,
 "Three separate things. Many tables: <code>GameServer</code> is a map from game id to game and holds no lock of its "
 "own, so the shard key is the game id and tables never contend. Out of turn: the game refuses with its own exception "
 "type, so the server can answer 409 rather than 500, and the refusal is checked before the die so it costs nothing. A "
 "retried request: the same roll must not move the token twice, so the request id is the key &mdash; "
 "<code>IdempotentRolls</code> plays it once and returns the same <code>Turn</code> to every later copy. If you want "
 "the gate without a lock at all, <code>TurnToken</code> keeps whose turn it is in an <code>AtomicReference</code> and "
 "a player moves by winning a compare-and-set. The honest caveat is that in one process a crash between the roll and "
 "the remembering loses the record; the database version below fixes that properly.",
 X("online play", "persistence")),
("Persist it. And now there are two servers.", "twist", 5,
 "The positions and the log go behind a repository interface and the game's code does not change, only what it was "
 "handed. The turn number becomes the version: a turn is written only if the stored game is still on the turn the "
 "caller thinks it is on, which is <code>UPDATE game SET turn_no = turn_no + 1 ... WHERE game_id = ? AND turn_no = ?</code> "
 "&mdash; zero rows updated means somebody else moved first, so re-read and try again. That is the database doing "
 "exactly what the <code>ReentrantLock</code> did in one process, and it works across as many servers as you like. The "
 "turn row carries the request id with a unique index on it, so an at-least-once delivery applies once.",
 X("persistence", "deterministic replay")),
("Where does time come from, and how do you make a player who has not rolled in thirty seconds lose the turn?", "design", 3,
 "The game has a <code>Clock</code> it was handed and stamps every turn with it; nothing else reads the wall clock. A "
 "test hands in a clock that returns a fixed instant and moves it by hand, so thirty seconds pass in no time at all. "
 "The timer itself is not in the game: it is a small object wired in as an observer, so every turn tells it who is on "
 "the clock now, and either a sweeper or the next request asks it whether that player is late. When they are, it calls "
 "<code>forfeitTurn</code>, which takes the same lock, writes a turn row with a roll of 0 and rotates the queue "
 "&mdash; a forfeit is a turn, so it is in the log and in the replay like everything else.",
 X("turn timers", "online play")),
("Which pattern is where, and where would a Factory or a Builder earn its place?", "design", 8,
 "None of them was chosen up front; each is what a move produced. Strategy is move 3: the dice, the overshoot rule and "
 "the extra-turn rule are the three things that will change. Decorator is the same move's wrapper, "
 "<code>CappedExtraTurn</code>. Observer is move 4's rule that a screen must never be inside the lock. State is move "
 "6, the game's life, which is why a roll after the win is refused. Command is move 6 again: the <code>Turn</code> "
 "record carries from, to and the jump, so it is an audit row, a replay script and an undo log at once. Builder is the "
 "interesting one &mdash; on most LLDs it is ceremony and you should say so, but here it earns its place: seven "
 "optional knobs, four real invariants, one choke point, and a package-private constructor so an invalid game cannot "
 "exist. Factory has not earned one: the builder's <code>dice(count, faces)</code> is already the registry, and it "
 "earns the name the day rules arrive as configuration strings. Singleton earned nothing at all.",
 sect(src, "final class GameBuilder {", "final class GameServer {")),
("Which SOLID letter is where in this code?", "design", 5,
 "S: the board resolves a landing, the dice makes a number, the game drives a turn, the builder assembles &mdash; each "
 "has one reason to change. O: <code>ReachOrPass</code> is a new file and one builder line, and the turn loop never "
 "learns about it. L: the game calls <code>dice.roll()</code> and never asks which dice it got, which is precisely why "
 "a scripted die makes tests exact. I: five interfaces with one method each, so a fake is a lambda. D: the game "
 "depends on <code>Dice</code>, <code>MoveStrategy</code>, <code>ExtraTurnRule</code> and <code>Clock</code>, all "
 "handed in by the builder, which is why a test can hand it a die that throws 6, 6, 2, a clock stuck on last Tuesday "
 "and a move rule that deliberately returns nonsense.",
 "// S: one reason to change each\n"
 "Jump   jumpAt(int cell)                       // Board: what is on a cell\n"
 "int    roll()                                 // Dice: a number\n"
 "Turn   roll(String playerId)                  // Game: the turn loop, and the lock\n\n"
 "// O + L: a new rule is a new file; the caller never asks which one it got\n"
 "final class ReachOrPass implements MoveStrategy {\n"
 "    public int landingCell(int from, int roll, int size) { return Math.min(from + roll, size); }\n"
 "}\n"
 "new GameBuilder().moveRule(new ReachOrPass())         // the only line that changes\n\n"
 "// I: one method each, so a test double is a lambda\n"
 "interface Dice          { int roll(); }\n"
 "interface MoveStrategy  { int landingCell(int from, int roll, int boardSize); }\n"
 "interface ExtraTurnRule { boolean rollAgain(int roll, int extrasSoFar); }\n"
 "interface GameObserver  { void onTurn(String gameId, Turn t); }\n"
 "interface Clock         { long nowMs(); }\n\n"
 "// D: everything is handed in, which is what makes the failure tests possible\n"
 "new GameBuilder().dice(new ScriptedDice(6, 6, 2)).clock(() -> now[0])\n"
 "                 .moveRule((from, roll, size) -> size + 99)   // deliberately broken, in a test\n"),
]

